From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:53:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Retain forwards-filling animations and add script animations

`process_animations_for_style` removed every finished animation from the
element's set. For `animation-fill-mode: forwards` or `both` that throws
away a contribution the element is still entitled to: the value is right
in the resolve the animation finishes in, and wrong on the next restyle
of that element, whatever triggered it. Anything whose only visible
state came from a fill snapped back to its cascaded value. For example,
a lazy loader that fades images in over a base `opacity: 0` goes
transparent again the first time the page writes a style.

`get_property_declaration_at_time` already honours the fill, so keeping
the animation in the set is enough; it is retired the usual way when the
element stops specifying it. Fold the duplicated fill-mode predicate
into `Animation::fills_forwards`, since both call sites need it.

Cancelling has to mark the set dirty, or the fill can never be removed.
Dropping `animation-name` sets `AnimationState::Canceled`, but
`process_animations` only recascades when `ElementAnimationSet::dirty`
is set, and this loop never set it, while `cancel_active_transitions`
and `cancel_all_animations` both do. Mark it dirty in the same resolve,
before `clear_canceled_animations` sweeps the entry and the set
disappears.

Web Animations (Element.animate) need the same cascade as CSS
animations: keyframes computed against the element's style, implicit end
keyframes, per-keyframe easing, and interpolation into the Animations
level. The servo build only knows CSS animations, which it times itself
from the animation-* properties. A ScriptAnimation carries keyframes and
a progress the embedder sets. stylo builds its computed keyframes during
the traversal (rebuilding when the element's non-animation rules change)
and samples it after the element's CSS animations, which is their
composite order.

The keyframe easing epsilon matches Blink's kBezierEpsilon: solving more
tightly shifts the sixth digit pages read back from getComputedStyle.

Co-authored-by: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
